    fix(bash): let Stop end a command whose output a survivor holds open (#1016)
    
    * fix(bash): let Stop end a command whose output a survivor holds open (#647)
    
    On Stop, pi's local shell backend kills the command's process tree
    (process-group SIGKILL on POSIX, `taskkill /T` on Windows) and then waits
    for every inherited stdout/stderr handle to fall idle, re-arming a 100ms
    grace timer on each chunk (waitForChildProcess, earendil-works/pi#5303).
    A descendant the kill cannot reach keeps that wait alive for as long as
    it writes: a process in its own session on POSIX, or an orphan whose
    parent already exited on Windows, which `taskkill /T` cannot follow
    (earendil-works/pi#9129). The bash tool call then never returns, so the
    Stop request, the Stop button and any queued steering all wait until
    the script finishes on its own.
    
    Pi Web already routes agent and `!` shell commands through its own
    BashOperations wrapper, so it now bounds that wait there: once the
    signal aborts, pi's normal path gets one second to settle (it takes
    well under that when the tree is gone) before the wrapper rejects with
    pi's own "aborted" error, which the bash tool reports as
    "Command aborted" and the `!` executor as a cancelled run. Output that
    arrives after the release is dropped instead of being appended to a
    result that has already been finalized. The unreachable process itself
    keeps running; killing it needs an upstream change.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * fix(chat): say when a steering message is delivered (#647)
    
    The Steer button's tooltip promised to "interrupt the current run and
    inject this message now". Steering does not interrupt anything: pi
    queues the message and delivers it after the current assistant response
    and its tool calls finish, before the next model call (pi docs
    rpc-commands.md, sdk.md). A message steered during a long Python run
    therefore waits for the script, which the tooltip gave no reason to
    expect. Describe the real timing and point to Stop, which is what
    interrupts the running tool calls.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * fix(bash): release a timed-out command the same way as a stopped one (#647)
    
    pi's `timeout` kills the process tree and then waits on the inherited
    output handles exactly like Stop does, so a descendant the kill cannot
    reach kept the tool call pending past its timeout with no "Command timed
    out" result. The wrapper now also releases the command one grace period
    after the timeout, with pi's own `timeout:<seconds>` error.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
